Given: \[G_{1}(s)=\frac{1}{s^{2}+as+b}
\qquad \text{= CLTF}\] \[G_{2}(s)=\frac{s}{s^{2}+as+b}
\qquad \text{= CLTF}\] The characteristic equation for \(G_1(s)\) is given by, \[s^{2}+as+b=0\] Roots of above characteristic equation is given by, \[s_{1},s_{2}
=
\frac{-a\pm\sqrt{a^{2}-4b}}{2}\] \[G_{1}(s)
=
\frac{1}
{
\left(
s+\dfrac{a}{2}-\dfrac{\sqrt{a^{2}-4b}}{2}
\right)
\left(
s+\dfrac{a}{2}+\dfrac{\sqrt{a^{2}-4b}}{2}
\right)
}\] \[G_{1}(s)
=
\frac{1}{(s-\omega_{L})(s-\omega_{H})}\] \(G_{1}(s)\) represents product of transfer functions of two low pass filters with cut-off frequencies of \(\omega_{L}\) and \(\omega_{H}\). Thus \(G_{1}(s)\) represents a low pass filter with two poles. When a system has multiple poles, the dominant pole (i.e. nearest \(j\omega\)-axis) given overall cut-off frequency of transfer functions \(G_{1}(s)\) is \[\omega_{o}
=
\frac{-a+\sqrt{a^{2}-4b}}{2}\] 3-dB bandwidth of system is, \[BW=\omega_{o}
=
\frac{-a+\sqrt{a^{2}-4b}}{2}\] \[G_{2}(s)
=
\frac{s}
{
\left(
s+\dfrac{a}{2}-\dfrac{\sqrt{a^{2}-4b}}{2}
\right)
\left(
s+\dfrac{a}{2}+\dfrac{\sqrt{a^{2}-4b}}{2}
\right)
}\] \[G_{2}(s)
=
\frac{s}{(s-\omega_{L})(s-\omega_{H})}\] \(G_{2}(s)\) represents a bandpass filter with lower and upper cut-off frequencies \(\omega_{L}\) and \(\omega_{H}\) respectively. Thus \(G_{2}(s)\) represents a bandpass filter with two poles. \[\omega_{L}
=
\frac{
a-\sqrt{a^{2}-4b}
}{2}\] \[\omega_{H}
=
\frac{
a+\sqrt{a^{2}-4b}
}{2}\] 3-dB bandwidth is given by, \[BW=\omega_{H}-\omega_{L}\] \[BW
=
\left(
\frac{
a+\sqrt{a^{2}-4b}
}{2}
\right)
-
\left(
\frac{
a-\sqrt{a^{2}-4b}
}{2}
\right)\] \[BW=\sqrt{a^{2}-4b}\] Hence, none of the options is correct. Nearest answer is option (C). \[\boxed{\text{(C) }\sqrt{a^2 - 4b},\, \sqrt{a^2 - 4b}}\]

Given, \[A=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0 &amp; 1\\
-1 &amp; 0
\end{array}\right]\] State transition matrix is given by, \[\phi(t)=\mathcal{L}^{-1}\left\{(sI-A)^{-1}\right\}\] First compute, \[sI-A=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
s &amp; -1\\
1 &amp; s
\end{array}\right]\] Determinant is, \[|sI-A|
=
s^2+1\] Therefore, \[(sI-A)^{-1}
=
\frac{1}{s^2+1}
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
s &amp; 1\\
-1 &amp; s
\end{array}\right]\] \[=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\dfrac{s}{s^2+1} &amp; \dfrac{1}{s^2+1}\\ \dfrac{-1}{s^2+1} &amp; \dfrac{s}{s^2+1}
\end{array}\right]\] Taking inverse Laplace transform, \[\phi(t)
=
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\cos t &amp; \sin t\\
-\sin t &amp; \cos t
\end{array}\right]\] \[\boxed{\text{(A) }\begin{bmatrix}\cos t &amp; \sin t\\ -\sin t &amp; \cos t\end{bmatrix}}\]
